Q: Is there any reason why NPM doesn&#39;t use .nvm if it exists? A: <p><a href="https://github.com/nvm-sh/nvm" rel="nofollow noreferrer">NVM</a> is a separate project not affiliated with <a href="https://www.npmjs.com" rel="nofollow noreferrer">NPM</a>.</p>
<p>NPM will warn you if you specify an NPM and/or Node version in its <em>own</em> files, <code>.npmrc</code> and <code>package.json</code>.</p>
<p><code>.npmrc</code> (project-level or globally):</p>
<pre class="lang-ini prettyprint-override"><code>engine-strict=true
</code></pre>
<p><code>package.json</code> (project-level):</p>
<pre><code>{
  &lt;...&gt;
  &quot;engines&quot;: {
    &quot;node&quot;: &quot;22.18.0&quot;,
    &quot;npm&quot;: &quot;&gt;=10.9.3&quot;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>For example, you might get output like:</p>
<pre><code>npm warn EBADENGINE Unsupported engine {
npm warn EBADENGINE   package: 'my-project@1.0.0',
npm warn EBADENGINE   required: { node: '22.18.0', npm: '&gt;=10.9.3' },
npm warn EBADENGINE   current: { node: 'v20.18.2', npm: '10.8.2' }
npm warn EBADENGINE }
</code></pre>

Q: Detect if Asp.Net form is valid with javascript/jquery A: <p>Okay I found a solution. Page_ClientValidate() does the trick.</p>
<pre><code>function showLoading() {
    if (Page_ClientValidate()) { 
        $(&quot;#loader&quot;).show(); }
    else { 
        alert(&quot;Form is invalid!&quot;);
    }
}
</code></pre>
